  "executive_summary": "This paper addresses the fundamental limitation of terahertz (THz) generation efficiency in nonlinear metasurfaces, specifically those based on split-ring resonators (SRRs). The authors identify that linear radiation damping and dissipative losses significantly reduce the local near-field amplitude, thereby limiting the second-order nonlinear response. To overcome this, they propose a novel concept combining a resonant dark-state metasurface with a specific spatial symmetry. The design utilizes a thin silicon slab with metal walls to create quantized dark modes, specifically the TE2,0 mode, which provides high-quality-factor local field enhancement without direct coupling to incident radiation. Two gold SRRs are asymmetrically placed on the silicon slab to couple the pump radiation into this dark state via simultaneous electric and magnetic critical coupling, achieving near-perfect absorption. Crucially, the spatial arrangement ensures that the linear radiating moments of the SRRs interfere destructively, eliminating linear radiation damping, while the nonlinear components interfere constructively. Numerical simulations using a self-consistent FDTD calculation based on the hydrodynamic model demonstrate a giant second-order nonlinear susceptibility of approximately 2.4 × 10⁻¹¹ m/V. This represents an order-of-magnitude improvement over previously reported SRR metasurfaces (1.7 × 10⁻¹² m/V) and corresponds to a two-orders-of-magnitude enhancement in THz energy extraction. The study provides a paradigm for high-efficiency tunable nonlinear meta-devices, paving the way for revolutionary THz technologies and optoelectronic nano-circuitry by decoupling local field enhancement from radiative losses.",
